Managing Screen Time and Digital Well-Being
The internet is designed to be engaging, and excessive use can affect mental health, sleep, and productivity.
Learning to use the internet includes developing strategies for digital well-being. This means setting boundaries,
using built-in screen time tools, and taking regular breaks from screens.
Understanding the difference between active and passive internet use—such as engaging in a learning course versus mindlessly scrolling—helps users make intentional choices. 
Digital well-being is about using the internet as a tool rather than being controlled by it.
